00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:44.340
So without further
ado, let's get

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.180
to answering your questions.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:48.630
The first question is
about colds and flus.

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.760
And the question
specifically is,

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:54.810
why is it that we get more colds
and flus in the winter months?

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.340
Is it the temperature outside?

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.800
Is it the fact that people are
spending more time indoors?

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.040
Or is it a myth that we get more
colds and flus in the winter

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:02.640
months?

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:05.520
Well, the first thing is that
the research very clearly

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.310
shows that in winter
months there is a greater

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:10.470
prevalence of colds and flus.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.750
Now, of course, the words,
"in the winter months"

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:14.400
means many things.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:18.170
For instance, in most areas
of the world unless you

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:21.080
live directly near the
equator, in the winter

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:24.030
months means that days
are going to be shorter

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:27.320
and nights are longer
than in the summer months.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:28.790
In general, that's true.

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:30.380
It's of course,
especially true if you

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:31.940
live very far from the equator.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:35.390
So imagine somebody living up
in Tromsø, Norway, which is

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:37.130
very close to the North Pole.

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:41.150
In winter, days are very,
very short in Tromsø.

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:43.200
And conversely in
the summer months,

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.740
days are very, very
long in Tromsø Norway.

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:49.940
Now, Tromsø Norway represents an
extreme of day length variation

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:51.650
according to time of year.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:53.210
The closer you get
to the equator,

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:56.570
the less variation there is in
day length and therefore night

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:58.290
length across the year.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:00.410
However, in most
locations on Earth,

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:04.040
days will be significantly
shorter by about an hour or two

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:06.900
at least for certain
parts of the year.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.050
And in general,
shorter days correlate

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:10.610
with colder temperatures.

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:12.420
That's what's really
important to know,

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:15.930
which is that shorter days
generally correlate with colder

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:19.870
temperatures regardless of where
you live on the planet Earth.

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:20.370
OK.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:23.040
So in the winter months
as we're calling it,

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:26.850
involves at least two things,
shorter days, which means what?

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:30.390
It means less sunlight
and either more artificial

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:33.570
light or more darkness depending
on how much artificial light

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:34.780
you rely on.

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:38.190
It also means, as we
mentioned, colder temperatures.

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:40.500
Colder temperatures, of
course, doesn't necessarily

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.050
mean that you are exposed
to those colder temperatures

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:44.790
because what's the third thing?

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:46.830
Shorter days and
colder temperatures

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:50.620
outside mean that people are
spending more time indoors.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:52.350
So what the research
clearly shows

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:55.260
is that one of the reasons,
not the only, but one

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:58.050
of the reasons for the greater
prevalence of colds and flus

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:00.210
in the short days a.k.a.

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:02.370
winter months of the
year is that people

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:04.650
are spending more time
indoors and therefore

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:07.875
at closer proximity, which
raises a whole bunch of really

00:04:07.875 --> 00:04:09.750
interesting questions
that have been explored

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:13.890
in the peer reviewed research
about proximity cold and flu

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:14.460
transmission.

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:15.960
There have been
studies for instance

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.252
where people come into the
laboratory who are suffering

00:04:18.252 --> 00:04:20.940
from cold and flu and are
sneezing, having them sneeze

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:23.250
at different proximity
to other people,

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:25.403
obviously people are
doing this under consent.

00:04:25.403 --> 00:04:26.820
They're agreeing
to participate in

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:28.200
these particular experiments.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:31.260
And there does seem to be
a very nice relationship

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:34.510
between physical
distance between you,

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:37.660
the uninfected person,
and the sneezing,

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:41.030
coughing, nose
wiping, eye wiping,

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:43.260
we'll get into why I
mentioned all of those things

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:46.230
in a moment, person that
is suffering from the flu.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:47.665
In other words,
the closer you are

00:04:47.665 --> 00:04:49.290
to somebody who's
sneezing or coughing,

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:52.210
the higher probability that you
will contract that cold or flu.

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:52.710
OK.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:54.430
So we've already
established two things.

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:57.930
First of all, physical
proximity is a key variable

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.420
in terms of transmission
of cold and flu.

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:02.275
And at some level that's a duh.

00:05:02.275 --> 00:05:04.650
I mean, if you think about
it, if you're in the next room

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:06.630
or down the hallway from
or across the street

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:08.160
from somebody with
a cold or flu,

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:10.620
you don't expect to contract
that cold or flu from them.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:12.390
Whereas if you're
in the same vehicle

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:15.300
with them or you're sitting
next to them on an airplane

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:18.450
or you are on the same bus or
in the same classroom as them

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:20.190
and they are sneezing
and coughing,

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:22.890
well, then, the probability that
you would get that cold or flu

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:24.180
from them is increased.

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:25.630
That's just obvious.

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:28.350
But the studies that have
explored the relationship

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:31.230
between physical proximity and
transmission of cold and flu

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:34.085
have actually analyzed things
down to the range of well,

00:05:34.085 --> 00:05:35.460
if you're standing
one foot apart

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:37.890
or you're standing three
feet apart or six feet apart,

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:40.710
what's the probability that you
will contract that cold or flu?

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:43.020
And it follows a very
direct relationship

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:45.247
where the closer the
proximity, the more likely

00:05:45.247 --> 00:05:46.830
that you're going
to contract the cold

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:47.830
or flu from that person.

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:51.090
And of course, anything like
exchange of skin contact

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:54.570
or exchange of saliva
because obviously in cases

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:57.060
of romantic relationships
where people will kiss

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:00.600
or in familial relationships or
friendships where people hug,

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:04.350
any contact also further
increases the transmission

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:05.100
of cold and flu.

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:08.470
Not just from stuff emitted
from the nasal passages

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:10.470
and from the mouth when
people sneeze and cough,

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:12.270
but also when people
wipe their eyes,

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:14.700
even when people wipe their
skin because oftentimes they

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:17.340
have cold or flu
virus on their skin.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.960
It doesn't always survive
terribly long on the skin.

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:23.250
But if they wipe their
face or wipe their nose

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:25.170
or are using tissues
and then don't

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:26.910
wash their hands
thoroughly afterwards,

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:29.490
indeed they can transmit
cold and flu simply

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:30.490
through handshake.

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:30.990
OK.

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:32.760
So for all you
hypochondriacs out there,

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:34.920
I'm arming you with a
lot of useful knowledge

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:37.560
that will justify
staying away from people

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:40.140
who perhaps have cold and flu.

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:42.900
Now, this particular point
about proximity and people

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:45.090
being indoors more
during the winter months,

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:47.430
and that's why there's more
cold and flu transmission,

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:50.100
raises a whole bunch of
interesting questions,

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:53.970
and, in fact, protocols that I
plan to cover in a Huberman Lab

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:56.970
podcast episode solely
devoted to colds and flus

00:06:56.970 --> 00:06:59.160
and how to avoid
getting colds and flus.

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:01.380
But one brief mention
or telegraph of what

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:03.330
I plan to cover
in that episode is

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:06.780
that if people are telling
you that they are sneezing

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:10.050
and coughing due to a cold or
flu but that they are quote

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:12.720
unquote "not contagious"
either because they

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:15.700
are early in the cold or flu
or late in the cold or flu

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:17.980
and they have these theories
or claims that oh, I'm

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:20.140
no longer contagious.

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:23.470
Keep in mind that any time
someone is sneezing or coughing

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:25.270
they are in fact contagious.

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:27.160
So I'll get back to
that in that full length

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:28.720
episode about colds and flus.

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:30.250
But I felt it was
too important not

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:33.130
to mention right now that
people's theories about when

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:34.720
they are quote
unquote "contagious"

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:38.050
or "not contagious" are
rarely substantiated

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:39.375
by the actual data.

00:07:39.375 --> 00:07:40.750
The actual data
point to the fact

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:42.542
that when people are
sneezing and coughing,

00:07:42.542 --> 00:07:46.310
if it's due to a cold or flu,
they are still contagious.

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:46.810
OK.

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:50.410
So we've established that
cold outside means people tend

00:07:50.410 --> 00:07:52.960
to be indoors more
which increases

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:55.000
physical proximity, which
is one of the reasons

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:57.040
why there's more cold
and flu transmission.

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:00.340
One of the other reasons
why being indoors more

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:02.710
tends to increase cold
and flu transmission

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:07.420
is that in the cold months of
winter when people are indoors,

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:09.910
they tend to be not
under air conditioning,

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:12.730
not at neutral temperatures,
but rather they

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:14.470
tend to be in heated rooms.

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:16.150
And depending on
the type of heating

00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:20.080
that's used, but in general,
due to all forms of heating,

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:22.930
the heated air tends
to be drier air.

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:24.730
Now, this is a little
bit counterintuitive

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:28.180
because if you ever go outside
on a really cold winter day,

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:30.340
you will realize
that the cold weather

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:33.190
outdoors is extremely dry.

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:36.169
You can almost quote unquote
"feel the dryness of the air."

00:08:36.169 --> 00:08:38.770
And if you can't quote unquote
"feel the dryness of the air"

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:41.350
or imagine what that's
like just imagine this,

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:44.830
think of yourself outdoors
on a very cold winter

00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:47.550
day taking a brisk walk.

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:50.280
Then think about yourself
taking that same brisk walk

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:52.410
on a very humid summer day.

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:54.540
Humidity is, of course,
the concentration

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:57.420
of water in the ambient
environment, the air,

00:08:57.420 --> 00:09:00.240
whereas the dryness is
the lack of humidity.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:03.000
And just that little simple
GedankenNet or thought

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:05.250
experiment will
remind you just how

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:09.570
dry the cold air is out of
doors during the winter months.

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:12.450
Whereas indoors we're
heating that air

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:15.090
and indeed the heating
of that air does

00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:18.540
have the property of drying
the nasal and oral passages

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:20.555
but especially the
nasal passages.

00:09:20.555 --> 00:09:21.930
So another key
reason why there's

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:24.330
more transmission of colds
and flus in the winter months

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:26.880
is because people are
spending more time indoors.

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:29.730
And oftentimes the way
those indoor environments

00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:32.400
are being heated is drying
out the nasal passages.

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:35.550
And the nasal passages
represent a primary site

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:39.300
of defense for viral
infections like colds and flus

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:41.940
but also bacterial infections
and fungal infections

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:42.910
for that matter.

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:46.170
Now, I've talked before on this
podcast about the importance

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:49.990
of using nasal breathing
under any conditions where

00:09:49.990 --> 00:09:52.130
you don't have to breathe
through your mouth.

00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:55.300
So if you are not eating,
if you're not speaking,

00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:57.917
or if you're not exercising
hard which by the way

00:09:57.917 --> 00:10:00.250
oftentimes requires that you
breathe through your mouth.

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:02.120
There's nothing wrong with
breathing through your mouth

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:03.610
if you're exercising
hard and you

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:05.027
need to breathe
through your mouth

00:10:05.027 --> 00:10:06.610
in order to bring
in enough oxygen.

00:10:06.610 --> 00:10:09.160
There are conditions under which
that's entirely appropriate.

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:11.327
You can learn more about
that in the podcast episode

00:10:11.327 --> 00:10:13.670
I did all about breathing
and breath work.

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:17.620
But for the most part it's
best to be a nasal breather

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:19.630
except under the conditions
I just mentioned.

00:10:19.630 --> 00:10:20.350
Why?

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:24.320
Well, the nasal passages contain
a number of physical barriers,

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:26.170
including the hairs
within your nose.

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:28.930
I know that hairs in
the nose, especially

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:30.910
if they are super
numerous are not

00:10:30.910 --> 00:10:32.365
considered aesthetically nice.

00:10:32.365 --> 00:10:33.740
People will trim
them, et cetera.

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:35.710
But those hairs in
your nose actually

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:38.020
serve as a barrier
toward infection.

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:40.030
This is well established.

00:10:40.030 --> 00:10:42.820
The lining of the nose, the
mucosal lining of the nose

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:44.210
contains a lot of things.

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:46.450
First of all, it acts as
its own physical barrier

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:49.758
and physical trap for incoming
viruses, fungal infections,

00:10:49.758 --> 00:10:50.800
and bacterial infections.

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:53.440
They literally get trapped
in the nasal passages.

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:56.572
And therefore can't enter
deeper into your physiology.

00:10:56.572 --> 00:10:58.030
And right now is
not the time to go

00:10:58.030 --> 00:10:59.572
into the whole
anatomy and physiology

00:10:59.572 --> 00:11:00.610
of the nasal passages.

00:11:00.610 --> 00:11:03.250
But keep in mind if
you saw the episode

00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:06.310
that I did with Noam Sobel
or you listen to the episode

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:08.500
that I did on olfaction,
both of those episodes

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:10.930
highlight the fact that
your brain sits not

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:12.530
far behind your nasal passages.

00:11:12.530 --> 00:11:14.993
There's a bony barrier there
called the cribriform plate,

00:11:14.993 --> 00:11:16.660
and there are some
other things as well,

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:19.700
but it's not far from your
nostrils to your brain.

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:21.970
And it is not far
from your nostrils

00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:24.350
to the rest of your
respiratory pathway, of course.

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:29.050
And so the hairs of your
nostrils, the mucus itself,

00:11:29.050 --> 00:11:31.990
and the microbiome,
the trillions

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:34.090
of little micro
bacteria that thrive

00:11:34.090 --> 00:11:36.850
in the mucosal lining of
your nasal passages actually

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:40.918
serve to protect against many
of the incoming infections.

00:11:40.918 --> 00:11:42.460
This is why it's so
important to keep

00:11:42.460 --> 00:11:46.058
the mucosal lining of your nasal
passages thriving and intact.

00:11:46.058 --> 00:11:46.850
How do you do that?

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:48.392
Well, one of the
best ways to do that

00:11:48.392 --> 00:11:50.710
is to make sure that the
air that you're breathing

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:53.397
is sufficiently humidified.

00:11:53.397 --> 00:11:55.480
So while there are many
different claims out there

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:57.093
about how to avoid
colds and flus,

00:11:57.093 --> 00:11:59.260
as long as we're having a
discussion about why there

00:11:59.260 --> 00:12:01.093
are more colds and flus
in the winter months

00:12:01.093 --> 00:12:03.640
because indeed there
are, it's worth

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:06.065
mentioning that if you
suffer from colds and flus

00:12:06.065 --> 00:12:07.690
and you're going to
spend a lot of time

00:12:07.690 --> 00:12:09.910
in particular
indoor environment,

00:12:09.910 --> 00:12:14.320
you might be wise to find a
air humidifier, something that

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:16.060
brings more moisture
into the air

00:12:16.060 --> 00:12:19.390
that you're breathing especially
at night while you're sleeping.

00:12:19.390 --> 00:12:22.425
Now, that's not going to prevent
the person in your environment

00:12:22.425 --> 00:12:23.800
who happens to
have a cold or flu

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:28.150
and is sneezing actively from
transmitting that cold or flu.

00:12:28.150 --> 00:12:30.612
But it will keep
your nasal passages

00:12:30.612 --> 00:12:32.320
and the rest of your
respiratory pathways

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:34.330
as healthy as they can
be and as resistant

00:12:34.330 --> 00:12:36.068
as they can be to
any colds or flues

00:12:36.068 --> 00:12:37.360
that you might be fighting off.

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:38.710
And this is another
key point, which

00:12:38.710 --> 00:12:40.030
is regardless of
whether or not it's

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:42.113
the winter months or the
other months of the year,

00:12:42.113 --> 00:12:44.980
all day long you're combating
different types of infections.

00:12:44.980 --> 00:12:46.995
Different types
of viral, fungal,

00:12:46.995 --> 00:12:49.120
and bacterial infections
are bombarding your system

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:51.820
and your immune
system counters that

00:12:51.820 --> 00:12:54.520
both through physical
and through neurochemical

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:57.380
and hormonal and pure
immune mechanisms.

00:12:57.380 --> 00:12:57.880
OK.

00:12:57.880 --> 00:12:59.490
There's a whole discussion
of this in the episode

00:12:59.490 --> 00:13:00.550
that I did on the immune system.

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:01.660
We'll do more on this.

00:13:01.660 --> 00:13:04.720
But keeping the air that
you breathe especially

00:13:04.720 --> 00:13:07.630
at night sufficiently
humidified is one great way

00:13:07.630 --> 00:13:11.770
to try and offset colds and
flus that you might be combating

00:13:11.770 --> 00:13:13.780
because you brought
that cold or flu

00:13:13.780 --> 00:13:17.600
home from work and your immune
system needs to ward it off.

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:18.100
OK.

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:20.290
If you are having a
hard time grasping

00:13:20.290 --> 00:13:22.420
how it is that you could
bring home a cold or flu

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:24.003
and then ward it
off, do you have it?

00:13:24.003 --> 00:13:24.670
Do you not have?

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:26.710
It is it sitting there trying
to get into your system?

00:13:26.710 --> 00:13:28.002
Or do you have it a little bit?

00:13:28.002 --> 00:13:30.070
Well, this is some
of the gray area

00:13:30.070 --> 00:13:32.660
around cold and flu and
viral transmission generally.

00:13:32.660 --> 00:13:34.160
I mean, we don't
know, for instance,

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:36.490
if you got a mild
sniffle as opposed

00:13:36.490 --> 00:13:39.010
to a really bad cold
or flu whether or not

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:41.920
it would have been a
really bad cold or flu,

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:45.650
had you slept less, had you
not humidified your air better,

00:13:45.650 --> 00:13:46.180
et cetera.

00:13:46.180 --> 00:13:47.317
Probably it would be.

00:13:47.317 --> 00:13:49.400
And if you've ever not
slept well for a little bit

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:51.710
and you're sick, you
experienced just how much worse

00:13:51.710 --> 00:13:53.000
that sickness feels.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:56.000
The symptoms of that sickness
go from very mild to moderate

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:58.280
or from mild to
moderate to very severe

00:13:58.280 --> 00:14:00.290
when you are sleep deprived.

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:04.310
That said, it's also clear that
some strains of the cold or flu

00:14:04.310 --> 00:14:06.660
can be more mild than other
strains of the cold or flu.

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:07.910
So this can be pretty nuanced.

00:14:07.910 --> 00:14:09.470
And we'll go deeper
into this in the episode

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:12.020
that I do on cold and flu and
how to avoid cold and flu.

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:15.980
But nasal breathing whenever
possible day and night time

00:14:15.980 --> 00:14:19.820
is extremely important for
encouraging the overall health

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:21.950
and resistance of
your respiratory

00:14:21.950 --> 00:14:25.190
pathways to incoming cold and
flu and other types of viruses.

00:14:25.190 --> 00:14:29.120
Similarly, humidifying the air
that you breathe especially

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:32.150
at night but perhaps also during
the day in your OFS environment

00:14:32.150 --> 00:14:35.180
or home environment can also be
very beneficial for warding off

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:36.080
colds and flus.

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:39.170
And if you'd like to read more
about the nasal versus mouth

00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:40.880
breathing components
of colds or flus,

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:43.545
I'll just mention one
paper now and again, I'll

00:14:43.545 --> 00:14:45.920
go into this in more depth in
the future episode on colds

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:46.490
and flus.

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:50.220
But I want to mention that
this is a bidirectional effect,

00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:53.210
meaning people who deliberately
breathe through their nose

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:56.060
tend to get fewer
colds and flus.

00:14:56.060 --> 00:14:58.550
Also people who tend to
breathe through their mouth

00:14:58.550 --> 00:15:01.580
more tend to get
more colds and flus.

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:04.010
Now, you might think that's
the same thing or just

00:15:04.010 --> 00:15:05.870
two sides of the same coin.

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:06.830
But actually it's not.

00:15:06.830 --> 00:15:09.950
There's what we call a
double dissociation whereby

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:11.780
if people are mouth
breathers, they

00:15:11.780 --> 00:15:14.720
are far more susceptible
to colds and flus

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:16.790
and other types of
respiratory illnesses.

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:18.620
I'll put a link to
one particular paper

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:21.050
that I like a lot in the
show note captions entitled

00:15:21.050 --> 00:15:23.570
association of oral breathing
with dental malocclusions

00:15:23.570 --> 00:15:25.205
and general health in children.

00:15:25.205 --> 00:15:27.080
This paper explores a
lot of different things

00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:29.485
about nasal versus mouth
breathing all of which,

00:15:29.485 --> 00:15:30.860
by the way point
to the fact that

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:34.220
nasal breathing whenever
possible is better for us

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:37.790
health wise, aesthetically,
dental health wise

00:15:37.790 --> 00:15:39.170
than is mouth breathing.

00:15:39.170 --> 00:15:41.450
But one of the key
components here and this is

00:15:41.450 --> 00:15:43.580
why I bring it up in the
context of this discussion

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:47.150
is quote "oral breathing is
related to a significantly

00:15:47.150 --> 00:15:50.240
higher prevalence of allergies
and a significantly more likely

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:54.300
getting sick and taking
medication for being sick."

00:15:54.300 --> 00:15:54.800
OK.

00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:57.380
So lots in this study,
lots in related studies.

00:15:57.380 --> 00:16:01.280
But nonetheless, being a nasal
breather, humidifying your air,

00:16:01.280 --> 00:16:03.110
paying attention
to whether or not

00:16:03.110 --> 00:16:06.680
you're indoors with people
who are coughing and sneezing

00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:08.643
because it's cold outside,
all of those things

00:16:08.643 --> 00:16:10.310
are going to be
relevant perhaps keeping

00:16:10.310 --> 00:16:11.960
a little bit of distance,
maybe a lot of distance

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:14.335
from those people or encouraging
them to wash their hands

00:16:14.335 --> 00:16:15.800
after they wipe their nose.

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:19.310
Maybe even sending them to a
little bit of not necessarily

00:16:19.310 --> 00:16:21.170
isolation although
that might be necessary

00:16:21.170 --> 00:16:23.870
but keeping them at a distance.

00:16:23.870 --> 00:16:26.180
I don't think it's too
much to ask somebody

00:16:26.180 --> 00:16:28.340
who's sick to not get you sick.

00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:31.550
Personally I think that's the
polite thing to try and do.

00:16:31.550 --> 00:16:33.902
And there's actually
not many things ruder

00:16:33.902 --> 00:16:35.360
than someone who
knows that they're

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:37.340
sick showing up to something
because they didn't want

00:16:37.340 --> 00:16:39.990
to miss that particular event
and getting everybody else sick

00:16:39.990 --> 00:16:41.420
frankly, that's my opinion.

00:16:41.420 --> 00:16:43.430
I think that opinion is
shared with many of you

00:16:43.430 --> 00:16:44.850
out there as well.

00:16:44.850 --> 00:16:47.270
Now, as I mentioned earlier,
when it's cold outside,

00:16:47.270 --> 00:16:48.530
the air tends to be drier.

00:16:48.530 --> 00:16:48.770
Not always.

00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:50.728
I mean, you could live
in an environment that's

00:16:50.728 --> 00:16:53.480
very cold and very rainy,
obviously very rainy

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:54.500
is very humid.

00:16:54.500 --> 00:16:57.720
It's maximum humidity
if you think about it.

00:16:57.720 --> 00:17:00.950
But if you are going
to spend time out

00:17:00.950 --> 00:17:04.859
of doors in cold days of
winter, which by the way,

00:17:04.859 --> 00:17:07.125
is a really good thing
for other reasons,

00:17:07.125 --> 00:17:08.750
I'll get into those
reasons in a moment

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:11.510
because they relate
directly to why there's

00:17:11.510 --> 00:17:13.099
such a prevalence
of colds and flus

00:17:13.099 --> 00:17:15.800
because in general,
people are not doing what

00:17:15.800 --> 00:17:17.030
I'm about to tell you to do.

00:17:17.030 --> 00:17:18.890
But there are two things
to keep in mind if you're

00:17:18.890 --> 00:17:19.932
going to be out of doors.

00:17:19.932 --> 00:17:23.660
First of all, the things
about proximity still hold.

00:17:23.660 --> 00:17:25.460
There have been
studies of people

00:17:25.460 --> 00:17:28.460
who have colds and flus
sneezing in indoor environments

00:17:28.460 --> 00:17:31.710
versus outdoor environments and
how much transmission there is.

00:17:31.710 --> 00:17:33.560
Again, it boils
down to proximity.

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:36.050
Yes, you can get a cold or
flu from somebody sneezing out

00:17:36.050 --> 00:17:37.760
of doors next to
you just as well

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:39.740
as you can if they
sneeze next to you

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:42.980
indoors There's a slightly
higher probability that you'll

00:17:42.980 --> 00:17:45.650
get it from them if they're
sneezing at that same distance

00:17:45.650 --> 00:17:49.280
but you're indoors as opposed
to outdoors obviously sneezers,

00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:51.650
coughers-- sneeze and cough
in the other direction,

00:17:51.650 --> 00:17:52.970
cover your mouth and nose.

00:17:52.970 --> 00:17:55.880
If you don't have a tissue
or something, we are told

00:17:55.880 --> 00:17:59.120
and I subscribe to the idea
that sneezing into your elbow

00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:02.330
or coughing into your elbow
is probably the next best

00:18:02.330 --> 00:18:06.030
thing to not covering up at all,
as opposed to into your hand,

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:08.300
which then you touch
other things and yourself.

00:18:08.300 --> 00:18:10.610
Anyway, without getting into
the mechanics of sneezing

00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:12.650
and coughing too
deeply, if you're

00:18:12.650 --> 00:18:14.720
going to be outside in
the cold winter months

00:18:14.720 --> 00:18:18.170
and you'd like to avoid getting
colds and flus, when you run,

00:18:18.170 --> 00:18:20.660
when you exercise,
when you walk,

00:18:20.660 --> 00:18:23.780
when you're in conversation,
try to nasal breathe unless you

00:18:23.780 --> 00:18:25.130
have to mouth breathe.

00:18:25.130 --> 00:18:29.810
And if you can sense your
nasal passages or your mouth

00:18:29.810 --> 00:18:32.090
drying out because of
that hard breathing,

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:33.800
and by the way exercise
during the winter

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:34.890
months is still important.

00:18:34.890 --> 00:18:37.340
So I'm not discouraging people
from exercising outdoors

00:18:37.340 --> 00:18:39.715
during the winter months as
long as you can do it safely.

00:18:39.715 --> 00:18:42.500
But if you feel those
air passages drying out,

00:18:42.500 --> 00:18:45.140
just be aware that when
you take those dried out

00:18:45.140 --> 00:18:47.623
air passages in doors and
you're around other people that

00:18:47.623 --> 00:18:49.040
might have colds
and flus, you are

00:18:49.040 --> 00:18:50.248
going to be more susceptible.

00:18:50.248 --> 00:18:54.380
So that's cause for taking a
hot shower, perhaps if you have

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:56.450
access to a steam
room or humidifier,

00:18:56.450 --> 00:19:01.200
and rehydrating those
nasal and oral passages.

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:03.770
Now, if you're
outside and running

00:19:03.770 --> 00:19:06.375
and you're breathing hard and
your nasal passages are drying

00:19:06.375 --> 00:19:08.750
out, does that mean that you're
more susceptible to colds

00:19:08.750 --> 00:19:10.667
and flus that are just
out in that environment

00:19:10.667 --> 00:19:11.720
if no one else is around?

00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:13.550
Chances are no.

00:19:13.550 --> 00:19:15.410
However, what we
were all told when

00:19:15.410 --> 00:19:18.380
we were kids which is if you
go outside and you get a chill,

00:19:18.380 --> 00:19:20.810
it's a good idea when
you come back inside

00:19:20.810 --> 00:19:22.460
to take a hot
shower or a hot bath

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:25.250
or if you have access to
it a sauna to heat back up.

00:19:25.250 --> 00:19:27.440
That's also true, why?

00:19:27.440 --> 00:19:29.660
Well, it does seem that
there's a relationship

00:19:29.660 --> 00:19:32.893
between core body temperature
and susceptibility

00:19:32.893 --> 00:19:33.560
to cold and flu.

00:19:33.560 --> 00:19:34.520
And this is something
I'm going to go

00:19:34.520 --> 00:19:36.710
deeply into in the
episode on colds and flus.

00:19:36.710 --> 00:19:40.790
Keep in mind that fevers that
are the response to exposure

00:19:40.790 --> 00:19:43.910
to a cold or flu or being full
blown sick with a cold and flu

00:19:43.910 --> 00:19:47.280
are an attempt of your immune
system to heat up that virus

00:19:47.280 --> 00:19:48.300
and destroy it.

00:19:48.300 --> 00:19:50.400
Keep that in mind if
you're considering

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:53.970
taking any medication to
lower your fever, of course,

00:19:53.970 --> 00:19:57.690
know that highly elevated
body temperatures a.k.a. fever

00:19:57.690 --> 00:20:00.190
can be very dangerous
to the brain and body.

00:20:00.190 --> 00:20:01.755
There's a fairly
limited upper range

00:20:01.755 --> 00:20:03.630
of temperatures that we
can go into before we

00:20:03.630 --> 00:20:04.740
start damaging ourselves.

00:20:04.740 --> 00:20:08.490
But also keep in mind that
fever is an adaptation designed

00:20:08.490 --> 00:20:11.380
to destroy viruses.

00:20:11.380 --> 00:20:13.230
So if you blunt the
fever, you are actually

00:20:13.230 --> 00:20:15.962
letting a cold or
virus to proliferate

00:20:15.962 --> 00:20:17.670
more readily than it
would if you allowed

00:20:17.670 --> 00:20:19.930
a slight fever to combat that.

00:20:19.930 --> 00:20:20.430
OK.

00:20:20.430 --> 00:20:22.650
So there's a whole discussion to
be had there because obviously

00:20:22.650 --> 00:20:24.630
you don't want people
cooking their brains

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:27.690
due to elevated fever and
not treating that fever.

00:20:27.690 --> 00:20:30.120
But you also don't want
to reduce your core body

00:20:30.120 --> 00:20:32.790
temperature too much which
raises the question of things

00:20:32.790 --> 00:20:33.990
like cold plunges.

00:20:33.990 --> 00:20:35.430
Should you be
taking cold showers

00:20:35.430 --> 00:20:37.320
and doing cold plunges
in the winter months

00:20:37.320 --> 00:20:39.722
when it's already cold and
you're susceptible to colds

00:20:39.722 --> 00:20:41.430
and maybe you're coming
down with a cold,

00:20:41.430 --> 00:20:43.030
should you do that?

00:20:43.030 --> 00:20:44.820
Well, that segues
into the next question

00:20:44.820 --> 00:20:47.700
that I'm going to answer
for this AMA, which relates

00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:50.700
directly to cold exposure
and so-called cyclic

00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:52.710
hyperventilation breathing
sometimes associated

00:20:52.710 --> 00:20:55.800
with so-called Wim Hof breathing
but other forms of deliberate

00:20:55.800 --> 00:20:56.770
breathing as well.

00:20:56.770 --> 00:20:59.430
And how specific forms of
deliberate breathing and how

00:20:59.430 --> 00:21:01.710
deliberate cold
exposure can indeed

00:21:01.710 --> 00:21:06.810
be used to offset or even
prevent entirely infection

00:21:06.810 --> 00:21:08.850
due to bacterial or viruses.

00:21:08.850 --> 00:21:11.040
But if you're
already coming down

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:12.930
with a bacterial
or viral infection,

00:21:12.930 --> 00:21:15.810
why cyclic hyperventilation
breathing or why deliberate

00:21:15.810 --> 00:21:18.780
cold exposure may actually
be the worst thing

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:22.500
if you are already contracting
or have contracted,

00:21:22.500 --> 00:21:26.430
if you are contracting or
have already contracted

00:21:26.430 --> 00:21:27.570
a cold or virus.

00:21:27.570 --> 00:21:29.760
So we'll answer
that question next.

00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:33.510
In the meantime, just know
that yes, indeed in the winter

